---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero';
import CrateGrid from '../components/CrateGrid';
import LifecycleDiagram from '../components/LifecycleDiagram';
import StressGauge from '../components/StressGauge';
import StackCard from '../components/StackCard.astro';
import SystemPlanes from '../components/diagrams/SystemPlanes.astro';
import HomeostaticLoop from '../components/diagrams/HomeostaticLoop.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container grid-bg">
    <Hero client:load />
  </div>

  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">System at a glance</span>
        <h2>One organism in three planes.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The agent runs at PID 1 inside a microVM. <code>corpus</code> is the
          only crate permitted to touch hardware directly. Every other crate
          forbids unsafe code. The human enters the system as one sensory input
          among others — never as the controller.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SystemPlanes />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="design-principles">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Design principles</span>
        <h2>Five constraints that shape every decision.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Anima is built on a small number of architectural commitments. Each one is
          stated as a constraint on the design, not an aspiration — they are checked
          at the crate level and enforced in CI.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="principle card">
          <span class="badge badge-accent mono">01</span>
          <h3>The agent owns its own body.</h3>
          <p>
            The agent runs at PID 1. It allocates its own memory tiers, schedules
            its own downtime, decides when to consolidate, and sequences its own
            execution loops — within human-defined policy bounds.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="principle card">
          <span class="badge badge-accent mono">02</span>
          <h3>Safety by quarantine.</h3>
          <p>
            Unsafe Rust is permitted only inside <code>corpus</code>, the privileged
            Trusted Computing Base. Every other crate declares
            <code>#![forbid(unsafe_code)]</code>. A PR that introduces
            <code>unsafe</code> outside <code>corpus</code> fails to compile.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="principle card">
          <span class="badge badge-accent mono">03</span>
          <h3>Lifecycle is structural, not optional.</h3>
          <p>
            Sleep, dreaming, and memory consolidation are first-class architectural
            elements — not performance optimisations to be skipped under load. An
            Anima system that never sleeps is a malfunctioning Anima system.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="principle card">
          <span class="badge badge-accent mono">04</span>
          <h3>The metaphor is load-bearing.</h3>
          <p>
            Anatomical terms are used because they describe the actual structure of
            the system: afferent input, efferent action, interoception, homeostasis.
            Engineers should be able to map a Rust function to a physiological role
            and back.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="principle card span-2">
          <span class="badge badge-accent mono">05</span>
          <h3>Bare-metal compliance.</h3>
          <p>
            No host OS dependencies in the production target. <code>smoltcp</code>
            for networking, <code>rustls</code> for transport security,
            <code>wasmtime</code> for sandboxing untrusted code, all compiled into
            a unikernel image suitable for Firecracker or Cloud Hypervisor.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent, rgba(125, 249, 200, 0.02));">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Workspace</span>
        <h2>Ten crates. One organism.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The workspace splits along functional rather than physiological lines.
          Each crate corresponds to one concern, and the boundary decisions are
          governed by three principles: a single privileged crate, separation of
          lifecycle from scheduling, and separation of input from policy.
        </p>
      </div>

      <CrateGrid client:visible />

      <div class="ws-foot">
        <a class="btn" href={withBase('/architecture')}>
          Full architecture spec →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle</span>
        <h2>A homeostatic loop, not a request handler.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A living system maintains its internal state within bounds by continuous
          feedback rather than by external command. Anima applies this to an LLM
          agent: rather than running flat-out until OOM-killed and restarting, the
          system observes its own pressures and adjusts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <LifecycleDiagram client:visible />

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <HomeostaticLoop />
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Interoception drives state</h3>
          <p>
            The stress index <code>s ∈ [0, 1]</code> combines latency degradation
            and context saturation. Transitions are driven by it together with the
            task agenda and explicit policy signals from
            <code>/dev/anima/senses/human</code>.
          </p>
          <StressGauge client:visible />
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>The waking loop in one read</h3>
          <pre><code>{`// crates/vita/src/loop.rs
pub async fn somatic_execution_loop(
    lifecycle: &mut LifecycleManager,
    monitor: &HomeostaticMonitor,
) -> Result<(), LifecycleError> {
    loop {
        let guidance = lifecycle.senses.read_active_bounds().await?;
        lifecycle.update_policy_bounds(guidance);

        let active = lifecycle.memory.get_l1_token_count();
        let stress = monitor.compute_systemic_stress_index(
            active, lifecycle.config.max_context,
        );

        if lifecycle.agenda.is_empty() && stress < 0.4 {
            lifecycle.transition_to_sleep_state().await?;
            continue;
        }

        if stress >= 0.9 {
            lifecycle.emergency_consolidate().await?;
            continue;
        }

        if let Some(task) = lifecycle.agenda
            .select_optimal_task(stress)
        {
            lifecycle.scheduler.dispatch_task(task).await?;
        } else {
            tokio::task::yield_now().await;
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent, rgba(93, 209, 255, 0.02));">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Stack</span>
        <h2>Performant, verifiable, and free of host-OS dependencies.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-3 stack-grid">
        <StackCard
          title="Async runtime"
          items={[
            { name: 'embassy', desc: 'no_std bare-metal async for corpus' },
            { name: 'tokio + tokio-util', desc: 'hosted dev layer and user-space daemons' },
          ]}
        />
        <StackCard
          title="Concurrent memory"
          items={[
            { name: 'scc', desc: 'lock-free concurrent hashmaps (L2 warm cache, praxis registry)' },
            { name: 'crossbeam', desc: 'unmanaged ring buffers (event bus, sensory streams)' },
          ]}
        />
        <StackCard
          title="Serialisation"
          items={[
            { name: 'rkyv', desc: 'zero-copy across process boundaries and snapshot hydration' },
            { name: 'postcard', desc: 'lightweight no_std messaging payloads' },
          ]}
        />
        <StackCard
          title="Vector storage"
          items={[
            { name: 'LanceDB', desc: 'embedded; deep vector indices and L3 archive' },
            { name: 'instant-distance', desc: 'in-memory HNSW for the warm path' },
            { name: 'TurboQuant TQ4', desc: '8× compression, rotation-based online quantisation' },
          ]}
        />
        <StackCard
          title="Network & security"
          items={[
            { name: 'smoltcp', desc: 'standalone event-driven TCP/IP — no host networking' },
            { name: 'rustls', desc: 'type-safe TLS, ring crypto provider for no_std' },
          ]}
        />
        <StackCard
          title="Sandbox & verification"
          items={[
            { name: 'wasmtime', desc: 'capability-secured, gas-metered WebAssembly runtime' },
            { name: 'kani', desc: 'bounded model checking of rate limiters and ring buffers' },
            { name: 'miri', desc: 'runtime validation of unsafe boundaries in corpus' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta card">
        <div class="cta-text">
          <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
          <h2>Get the workspace running locally.</h2>
          <p>
            The hosted kernel target runs as an ordinary Linux process and is
            sufficient for end-to-end exercises of all current subsystems. The
            microVM target is the production deployment.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cta-code">
          <pre><code>{`# clone the workspace
git clone https://github.com/codehalwell/animaos
cd animaos

# build the whole workspace
cargo build --workspace

# run the test suite
cargo test --workspace

# launch the hosted kernel
cargo run -p hosted --bin anima-hosted`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .section-head {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .section-head h2 { margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }

  .lede {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .principle {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .principle h3 {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  .principle p { margin: 0; }

  .principle.span-2 {
    grid-column: span 2;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bg-1), rgba(125, 249, 200, 0.04));
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .principle.span-2 { grid-column: 1; } }

  .ws-foot {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .stack-grid { gap: 1rem; }

  .cta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 3rem;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bg-1), var(--bg-2));
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .cta { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: 2rem; } }

  .cta h2 { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .cta-code pre { margin: 0; }
</style>

<script>
  // small smooth-scroll for anchors
  document.querySelectorAll('a[href^="#"]').forEach((a) => {
    a.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const href = a.getAttribute('href');
      if (!href || href === '#') return;
      const target = document.querySelector(href);
      if (target) {
        e.preventDefault();
        target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
      }
    });
  });
</script>
